PREAMBLE
The purpose of this license is to establish binding legal and technical parameters governing the submission of source code, documentation, and digital assets to the Poke Project. To safeguard the copyright integrity of the repository and ensure that all contributors possess complete technical comprehension of their submissions, this license restricts the use of generative artificial intelligence. Furthermore, because the Poke Project is hosted on Codeberg, and Codeberg does not allow LLMs (see: https://blog.codeberg.org/protecting-our-floss-commons-from-llms.html), strict compliance with upstream hosting prohibitions is required for all project participants.

TERMS AND CONDITIONS FOR CONTRIBUTION

0. Definitions.
a) "RFC Terminology" refers to the key words "MAY", "MUST", "MUST NOT", and "SHOULD", which are to be interpreted as described in RFC 2119.
b) "LLM / Generative AI Coding" refers to the automated synthesis of functional source code, scripts, documentation, or configuration files by neural network models or Large Language Models in response to natural language prompts or context windows.
c) "Vibecoding" refers to the practice of prompting a generative model to write, structure, or alter software while the human operator relies on iterative trial-and-error rather than writing and fully comprehending the underlying syntax, architecture, and execution logic.
d) "Deterministic Automation" refers to traditional software tools including static linters, CI/CD pipelines, and rule-based code formatters (such as Prettier or Black) that execute explicit, predictable rules rather than probabilistic generation.
e) "Generative AI Assets" refers to any non-text media, including images, video, audio, or 3D models, synthesized in whole or in part by machine learning algorithms (such as Midjourney, DALL-E, or Stable Diffusion).
f) "Contribution" refers to any source code, patch, script, configuration file, documentation, or asset submitted to the Poke Project via Pull Request, Merge Request, commit, or other communication channel.

1. Contributor Accountability and Warranty of Authorship.
Because artificial intelligence models lack legal personhood, ethical agency, and technical accountability, you MUST assume sole and complete responsibility for your Contribution. By submitting a Contribution to the Poke Project, you legally warrant and represent that you are the human author of the work, that you fully comprehend its execution logic, and that the Contribution complies with all project quality, security, and licensing standards.

2. Prohibition of Vibecoding and Generative AI Submissions.

a) General Ban and Upstream Compliance: Codeberg does not allow LLMs on its platform (https://blog.codeberg.org/protecting-our-floss-commons-from-llms.html).  you MUST NOT submit Contributions generated by Large Language Models. Vibecoding is strictly banned. All new logic, architectural scaffolding, tests, bug fixes, documentation, and boilerplate MUST be authored by a human.

b) Clause of Extreme Necessity and Mandatory Disclosure: You MUST NOT utilize AI assistance in the preparation of any Contribution unless an extraordinary, unavoidable technical necessity exists where all standard human development, research, and debugging methods have been completely exhausted. In the rare event that you must invoke this strict exception of extreme necessity, you are required to explicitly disclose both the exact model used and the verbatim prompt(s) provided to the model within your Pull Request or Merge Request description. Failure to provide the exact prompt and model identifier renders the Contribution invalid under this license.

c) Legacy Code Preservation: All LLM-generated code submitted and merged prior to June 3, 2026, under earlier policy versions, is grandfathered and will remain in the repository. Historical contributions will not be purged.

d) Permitted Local Usage: Provided that no generated code or text is submitted to the repository, contributors MAY use local AI tools solely to summarize or explain complex legacy codebases for personal comprehension, or to locally analyze human-written code for potential flaws prior to manual human remediation.

e) Prohibited Media and Data Leakage: You MUST NOT submit Generative AI Assets of any kind (images, video, audio, or 3D models) or AI-generated documentation. Furthermore, you MUST NOT input proprietary, unreleased, or sensitive Poke Project data into external AI models that retain inputs for training purposes.

3. Enforcement, Review Standards, and False Positives.
a) Prohibition of Automated AI Detectors: The Poke Project strictly prohibits the use of automated "AI detection" software to enforce this license. Such tools are statistically unreliable and frequently misclassify neurodivergent phrasing, non-native English writing, or structured coding styles as machine-generated. Contributors will never be penalized based on third-party detection scores.

b) Standard of Technical Review: Project maintainers MUST NOT reject Contributions, block Merge Requests, or interrogate contributors based on subjective suspicions or stylistic impressions that code "looks generated." If a reviewer reasonably suspects that a contributor has engaged in Vibecoding or does not understand their Contribution, the reviewer SHOULD ask direct technical questions regarding the implementation logic to verify human comprehension.

c) External Code Integration: When integrating human-written code from external free software repositories, wikis, or forums, you MUST verify that the snippet complies with compatible free software licenses, was not generated by an LLM, and that you fully understand its operation.

4. Community Conduct and Harassment Prohibition.
All technical reviews, discussions, and feedback MUST remain strictly focused on the technical merit, security, and quality of the Contribution. Community members MUST NOT harass, mock, or publicly accuse contributors regarding suspected AI usage. Suspected violations of this license MUST be handled professionally by project maintainers through technical review or reported privately through designated project channels. Hostility or dogpiling toward contributors constitutes a severe breach of the Code of Conduct.

5. Automated Approvals and Merge Authority.
Artificial intelligence tools MUST NOT be granted authority to make substantive or final decisions regarding the acceptance or merging of a Contribution. Only an authorized human maintainer may approve and merge a Contribution. This clause does not restrict the use of Deterministic Automation for objective technical validation, such as automated test suites, static analysis, and CI/CD pipelines.

6. Large-Scale Deterministic Initiatives.
Large-scale, automated deterministic initiatives (such as mass-refactoring bots) are not governed by the generative AI restrictions of this license, but MUST receive prior evaluation and formal approval from the project council before deployment.

7. Violations and Remedies.

Adherence to the terms of this license is mandatory for all contributors. Failure to comply will result in the following enforcement actions:

a) Any Pull Request or Merge Request found to contain unauthorized LLM-generated code, Vibecoding, undisclosed AI assistance, or Generative AI Assets submitted after June 3, 2026, WILL be immediately closed and rejected.

b) Contributors who repeatedly violate the terms of this license MAY be subject to temporary or permanent revocation of repository access and contribution privileges.

c) Breaches of Section 4 regarding contributor harassment will result in immediate disciplinary action under the project Code of Conduct.


